API stands for Application Programming Interface
API is used to communicate multipe applications
An API key is used to identify, secure, and control how an API is used.
Authentication (Who are you?), Security, Usage Tracking, Billing & Limits [often have limits (e.g., 1000 requests/day)]

It defines a set of rules and protocols, which allows one program to request data or services from another and
returns a response (usually in JSON or XML format)

-> Types of APIs
Web APIs (used over the internet)
REST APIs (most common)
SOAP APIs (older, more strict)
Library APIs (used within programming languages)

Example 1:
Zomato does not create its own maps. Instead, it uses the Google Maps API from Google. This helps Zomato show the location and track the delivery for customers. Google does not give full access to its system. It only allows limited data that Zomato needs. This is controlled using an API, and security is maintained using an API key.

Example 2:
When a user signs up on a new website, they may see options like “Sign up with Google” or “Sign up with LinkedIn”. This is done using an API. The website uses Google or LinkedIn to verify the user’s identity. Since these platforms already have verified user data, the new website does not need to check everything from the beginning. It simply uses the basic details provided and creates an account for the user.

